data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" French Revolution (1789–1799) was a watershed moment","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" in modern European history. It transformed the political, social, and cultural landscape of France, dismantled the absolute monarchy and feudalism","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", and inspired radical democratic movements worldwide. To understand how a stable, traditional society was upended so violently, one must examine the deep","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"-seated structural causes that ignited the revolution and the profound consequences that reshaped the world.\n\n### Causes of the French Revolution\n\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The eruption of the French Revolution was not the result of a single event, but rather a convergence of long-term economic, social,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" intellectual, and political pressures.\n\n1. **Social Inequality and the Ancien Régime**\nFrance under the *","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"Ancien Régime* (Old Regime) was rigidly divided into three legal categories known as Estates. The First Estate (cler","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"gy) and the Second Estate (nobility) enjoyed immense privilege, owned vast amounts of land, and were largely exempt from paying direct","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" taxes. Meanwhile, the Third Estate—comprising everyone else, from wealthy merchants and urban laborers to the vast majority of peasants—bore","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the overwhelming burden of taxation. Despite making up roughly 98% of the population, the Third Estate had virtually no","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" political voice, creating deep resentment against aristocratic privilege.\n\n2. **Economic Crisis and Financial Ruin**\nBy the late","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" 1780s, France was on the brink of bankruptcy. This was largely due to decades of reckless royal","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" spending, an inefficient and corrupt tax collection system, and the crippling financial costs of foreign wars, particularly France’s involvement in the American","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Revolution. To make matters worse, a series of severe droughts, harsh winters, and poor harvests in 1788","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" and 1789 led to acute food shortages. The price of bread—the staple food of the common people","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"—skyrocketed, leaving urban workers and peasants starving and desperate.\n\n3. **The Influence of the Enlightenment**\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The 18th century brought the Enlightenment, an intellectual movement that challenged traditional authority, divine right monarchy, and the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" power of the Catholic Church. Thinkers like Voltaire, Jean-Jacques Rousseau, and Montesquieu advocated for reason, individual","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" liberty, religious tolerance, and the social contract. Their ideas circulated widely among the literate middle class (the bourgeoisie), who","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" began to question the legitimacy of absolute monarchy and feudal privileges, arguing that government should derive its power from the consent of the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" governed.\n\n4. **Political Incompetence and Deadlock**\nKing Louis XVI was a weak and indecisive ruler,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" and his wife, Marie Antoinette, was widely reviled for her extravagant lifestyle amid national poverty. Recognizing the financial catastrophe, the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" monarchy attempted to implement tax reforms. However, the nobility resisted, demanding the convocation of the Estates-General—an advisory","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" assembly that had not met since 1614. When the Estates-General finally met in May 1789,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" it deadlocked over voting procedures, setting the stage for a revolutionary confrontation.\n\n---\n\n### Consequences of the French Revolution\n\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The revolution unfolded in violent, rapid phases, including the Reign of Terror, before culminating in the rise of Napoleon Bonaparte. Its","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" consequences were transformative, extending far beyond the borders of France.\n\n1. **Destruction of Feudalism and the Class","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Structure**\nOne of the immediate consequences of the revolution was the legal abolition of feudalism, noble titles, and class","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" privileges. The *Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen* (1789) proclaimed that all men are born free and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" equal in rights, emphasizing liberty, property, security, and resistance to oppression. Although true social and economic equality remained elusive","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", legal discrimination based on birth was permanently eradicated.\n\n2. **Redistribution of Power and the Rise of Democracy","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"**\nThe absolute monarchy was replaced first by a constitutional monarchy, and later by the First French Republic. The revolution established the radical","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" concept that sovereignty resides not in a monarch by divine right, but in the nation and its people. It introduced modern democratic","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" principles, mass political participation, nationalism, and secularism into European politics.\n\n3. **The Reign of Terror and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Radicalization**\nThe revolution did not follow a peaceful path. External threats from neighboring monarchies, counter-revolutionary uprisings","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" within France, and severe economic instability radicalized the movement. Under the leadership of Maximilien Robespierre and the Committee","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of Public Safety, the Reign of Terror (1793–1794) ensued, leading to the execution of King","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Louis XVI, Queen Marie Antoinette, and tens of thousands of suspected enemies of the revolution by guillotine. This phase stained","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the democratic ideals of the revolution with mass violence and established a precedent for the use of state terror.\n\n4. **The Rise","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of Napoleon Bonaparte**\nThe political instability of the Directory paved the way for a military coup in 1799 led by General","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Napoleon Bonaparte. Initially declaring himself First Consul and later Emperor, Napoleon preserved many core achievements of the revolution—such as the abolition of feudal","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"ism, meritocratic public administration, and the *Napoleonic Code* (a comprehensive legal system)—while centralizing authoritarian","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" control.\n\n5. **Global Geopolitical Impact**\nThe revolutionary and Napoleonic Wars spread the ideals of liberty, nationalism, and legal","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" reform across Europe. Even when monarchies were temporarily restored after Napoleon's defeat in 1815, the genie was","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" out of the bottle. The revolution inspired the Haitian Revolution (the successful anti-colonial slave revolt), fueled 19th-century","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" independence movements in Latin America, and laid the ideological groundwork for modern liberal democracies and socialist movements alike.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" French Revolution was a complex, contradictory event marked by high-minded ideals and horrific violence. By dismantling the structures of the *","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"Ancien Régime*, it shattered centuries-old traditions of hierarchy and tyranny, proving that ordinary people could fundamentally alter their political","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" and social destiny. Its legacy continues to define the modern political vocabulary of rights, citizenship, and revolution.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

